1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,908
Picture a county where 612 residents were referred for help.

2
00:00:05,008 --> 00:00:07,951
256 of them never got there.

3
00:00:08,851 --> 00:00:13,054
Most local health departments serve fewer than 50,000 people.

4
00:00:13,754 --> 00:00:17,070
Their directors are asked to prove what the community needs.

5
00:00:17,170 --> 00:00:20,682
But yearly numbers show need, not where people get stuck.

6
00:00:20,782 --> 00:00:24,540
So every year, the case starts over from a pile of spreadsheets.

7
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:28,402
CB-CAP. Know where care breaks. Show the proof.

8
00:00:30,102 --> 00:00:34,093
See where residents drop off, by kind of help, area and season.

9
00:00:36,293 --> 00:00:39,811
Small counts stay hidden, so no one can be identified.

10
00:00:41,611 --> 00:00:45,056
Every figure carries its source, its year and its range.

11
00:00:46,756 --> 00:00:49,279
Your plan becomes your yearly report.

12
00:00:49,379 --> 00:00:51,487
A person approves every word.

13
00:00:52,787 --> 00:00:56,796
Walk into the budget meeting with proof, not a pile of spreadsheets.

14
00:00:57,596 --> 00:01:01,729
Sources shown. Nobody identifiable. People decide.

15
00:01:02,629 --> 00:01:06,611
Want this for your county? Talk to the SozoRock Foundation.
